<svg class="faq-icon-plus flex-shrink-0" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M10 0C4.47719.0.0 4.47719.0 10c0 5.5231 4.47719 10 10 10 5.5231.0 10-4.4769 10-10C20 4.47719 15.5231.0 10 0zm0 18.7697c-4.82469.0-8.75-3.945-8.75-8.76974.0-4.82469 3.92531-8.75 8.75-8.75 4.8247.0 8.75 3.92533 8.75 8.75.0 4.82464-3.9253 8.76974-8.75 8.76974zM14.375 9.375h-3.75V5.625C10.625 5.28 10.345 5 10 5s-.625.28-.625.625v3.75H5.625C5.28 9.375 5 9.655 5 10s.28.625.625.625h3.75v3.75C9.375 14.72 9.655 15 10 15S10.625 14.72 10.625 14.375v-3.75h3.75C14.72 10.625 15 10.345 15 10S14.72 9.375 14.375 9.375z" fill="#000"/></svg></div></div><div data-faq-index=3 data-faq-expanded=false class="faq-item flex gap-4 items-start px-6 py-5 rounded-md border duration-200 cursor-pointer bg-gray-50 border-black-100/10"><div class="flex flex-col flex-1 gap-4"><div class="text-base font-bold text-black-100 md:text-lg">Can I automate web scraping jobs?</div><div class="faq-answer flex flex-col gap-3 text-sm text-black-100 md:text-base" style=display:none><p>Yes. You can automate scraping jobs by calling the API from your backend, scripts, scheduled tasks, no-code workflows, or data pipelines. ScrapingBee handles page fetching, JavaScript rendering, proxies, and anti-bot logic, so your automation can focus on when to run, which URLs to scrape, and where to send the data.</p></div></div><div class=flex-shrink-0><svg class="faq-icon-close flex-shrink-0" style="display:none" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M17.0711 2.92898c-3.9053-3.90522-10.23695-3.90522-14.14217.0-3.905437 3.90544-3.905216 10.23692.0 14.14212 3.90544 3.9055 10.23667 3.9055 14.14217.0 3.9052-3.9052 3.9054-10.23668.0-14.14212zM3.79889 16.2012C.387325 12.7896.401247 7.22443 3.81284 3.81283c3.41157-3.411566 8.96276-3.411566 12.37436.0 3.4116 3.41157 3.4116 8.96277.0 12.37437C12.7757 19.5988 7.21046 19.6127 3.79889 16.2012zm9.73661-3.5495L10.8839 10l2.6516-2.6516c.244-.24396.244-.63994.0-.8